<%# locals: (venture: nil) %>
<%# Hedged in the same way Fuime::TaxTrackerService is: Fuime is not a tax
    preparer and must not state a reader's obligations as fact. The threshold is
    read from that service rather than typed, so this page and the tracker cannot
    drift apart. %>

<p>
  A business can be profitable on paper and still die, because profit and cash
  are not the same thing. Profit is whether the work was worth doing. Cash is
  whether you can pay for the next job. Running out of the second kills you even
  when the first looks fine.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">How a profitable business runs out of money</h2>

<p>
  You buy materials in week one. You do the work in week two. The customer pays
  in week four. Between week one and week four you are out of pocket, and if
  three customers do that at once you cannot afford the materials for the fourth
  job even though all four are profitable.
</p>

<p>The ways out of that are not complicated:</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>Take a deposit before you buy anything.</li>
  <li>Get paid on the day, not "whenever suits".</li>
  <li>Do not take on a bigger job than you can afford to be out of pocket on.</li>
  <li>Keep a small buffer that you never spend, so one late payment is not an emergency.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Keep the business money separate</h2>

<p>
  Mixed up with your own spending, business money becomes impossible to think
  about. You cannot tell whether the business is working, and at tax time you
  cannot tell what was an expense.
</p>

<p>
  Keeping it separate is the single habit that makes everything else on this page
  easy. It is also the habit that makes the business feel real, which turns out
  to matter more than it should.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Records: boring, and it pays you back</h2>

<p>
  Write down every sale and every expense while you still remember what it was
  for, and keep the receipt. Not because somebody is going to check, though they
  might. Because an expense you did not record makes your profit look bigger than
  it was, and you may be taxed on the difference.
</p>

<p>
  A photo of a receipt in the right place takes four seconds. Reconstructing a
  year of them takes a weekend and you will get it wrong.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Tax, in plain terms</h2>

<p>
  Two different things get confused with each other constantly.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Sales tax</strong> is charged to your customer on what you sell, and
  passed on. It was never yours at any point, which is why treating it as income
  is expensive: you spend it, and then you owe it. Whether it applies depends on
  your state and on what you sell. Physical goods are commonly taxable, services
  often are not, digital products vary a lot. There is no national answer, and
  anyone who gives you one without asking where you live is guessing. Your
  state's department of revenue is the place to check, and it is worth checking
  before your first sale rather than after your hundredth.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Income tax</strong> is charged on what the business actually made,
  which is income minus legitimate expenses, not the total that passed through.
  This is the practical reason to record everything.
</p>

<p>
  Two things people assume that are not true. Being under 18 does not exempt you.
  Being paid in small amounts by individuals does not either.
</p>

<p>
  The line most likely to matter is self employment tax, which generally starts
  once net earnings from self employment reach
  <strong><%= number_to_currency(Fuime::TaxTrackerService::SELF_EMPLOYMENT_THRESHOLD_CENTS / 100.0) %></strong>
  for the year. Net earnings are slightly less than profit, which is why the
  tracker in your account watches that specific figure. You may also get a form
  in the post about payments processed for you. It is not a bill. It is a copy of
  what was reported, and you keep it with everything else.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">What to actually do</h2>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    Put a share of every payment aside on the day it arrives, somewhere you do
    not spend from. What share is a question for a tax professional. Having the
    habit costs you nothing either way.
  </li>
  <li>Record expenses with receipts, as they happen.</li>
  <li>
    Tell your parent or guardian early. Your business income is likely to touch
    their tax return as well as yours, and they should not find out in the same
    week it is due.
  </li>
  <li>Get a qualified person to look at it before anything is filed.</li>
</ul>

<% if venture.present? %>
  <p>
    <%= link_to "Your Tax Tracker adds this up as you go", fuime_taxes_path(event_slug: venture.slug), class: "underline" %>.
    It only sees what is in your ledger, so cash somebody handed you and income
    from anywhere else are outside it.
  </p>
<% end %>

<p class="muted text-sm">
  None of this is tax advice. It is the vocabulary you need to have a useful
  conversation with somebody who can give you some.
</p>
